# The exact `wolf --version` string that commit produces (first line; the
# binary also prints a second line naming its lupin pairing — 0.1.26 at this
# pin, up from 0.1.24 at v0.2.4). `lspconf doctor` fails on a mismatch: a stale
# local `wolf` producing green transcripts is the failure mode the pin exists
# to prevent.
#
# D57 decides the shape. A build is a release build only when the builder
# stamps BOTH `WOLF_COMMIT=<short sha>` and `WOLF_RELEASE=v{crate version}`,
# and the second is granted only when that exact tag points at HEAD. This pin
# is at v0.2.5, so a release build prints the BARE version and the pin clause.
# An unstamped build still prints `+dev.unknown` — the string any trunk build
# of the same crate version prints, which is exactly the stale-binary hole —
# so the pinned string remains a stamped build's, and `cargo xtask dist` in
# the submodule is the builder that computes the stamp (README §Running the
# server lane locally; its plain-cargo recipe cannot grant the release half).
#
# `xtask compat`'s `version_number` reads the number out of this string; it
# learned semver build metadata at le03 and takes the bare tag form
# unchanged — `0.2.5`, no `+` to strip. Asserted in xtask/src/compat.rs.
#
# SEVEN HEX DIGITS FOR THE FOURTH PIN RUNNING — and le08 finally has the
# CONSTANT. wolf-lang#199 is that D57's pin clause is clone-dependent: le05
# derived the mechanism as "`git rev-parse --short` sizes its AUTO abbreviation
# to the object count of the clone it runs in", le06 exhibited two clones on one
# box disagreeing, le07 re-ran it. All three left the threshold unstated, which
# made the defect sound like drift. It is a STEP FUNCTION, and the step is at
# **16,384 packed objects (2^14)** — measured, not read off a manual: three
# scratch repositories packed to 16,383 / 16,384 / 16,385 objects answer 7 / 8 /
# 8 hex digits (git 2.50.1). `git`'s auto-abbrev counts PACKED objects only, so
# the loose half of `count-objects -v` does not move it.
#
# The two clones on this box, at THIS commit:
#
#   * ~/GithubOrgs/wolffe-lang/wolf-lang — 12,257 packed — answers `6ade878`,
#     SEVEN, and the acquired binary agrees;
#   * this repo's `upstream/` submodule — 23,626 packed — answers `6ade878c`,
#     EIGHT.
#
# Their COMMIT counts are identical (1,020 each, `rev-list --all --count`), so
# the older shorthand "the bigger clone" is not the test either; the pack count
# is. And the consequence is worse than drift: the builder clone sits **4,127
# packed objects below the flip**, so an ordinary `git fetch` there will one day
# cross 16,384 and the string this file pins will become wrong on the builder's
# own box WITHOUT ANY COMMIT CHANGING. That is the argument for fixing #199
# upstream rather than re-measuring it every sprint — `--short=7`, or
# `core.abbrev`, in whatever computes the stamp.
#
# The line below records what the ACQUIRED artifact prints, because that is
# what CI acquires and what a user runs. A locally built submodule binary on
# this clone still fails `doctor` here, and that refusal is the pin doing its
# job. Filed as wolf-lang#199 — see docs/COMPAT.md.
